            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Later Works</strong><br>\nFollowing \"Jane Eyre,\" Brontë published several other novels, including \"Shirley\" in 1849 and \"Villette\" in 1853, which also discussed themes of women's independence and critique of social structures.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Marriage and Death</strong><br>\nBrontë married her father's curate, Arthur Bell Nicholls, in 1854 but died less than a year later on March 31, 1855, due to complications during her pregnancy.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nCharlotte Brontë is remembered as one of the most important English novelists, with her work influencing subsequent generations of writers and feminists. Her novels continue to be read and studied for their innovative treatment of the inner lives of women and their social commentary.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
